<body>
<div class="poster">

  <!-- HEADER -->
  <div class="header">
    <div class="venue">The Lancet Digital Health &mdash; AI in Clinical Medicine Symposium</div>
    <div class="poster-title">Artificial Intelligence in Clinical Decision-Making:<br>Performance, Safety, and Implementation Gaps</div>
    <div class="one-liner">A synthesis of current evidence on AI-assisted clinical decision support systems, highlighting diagnostic accuracy gains, safety considerations, and the translational gap from research to bedside deployment.</div>
  </div>

  <!-- ANCHOR STAT BANNER -->
  <div class="anchor-stat">
    <div>
      <div class="anchor-number">&lt;5%</div>
      <div class="anchor-sub-label">PROSPECTIVE VALIDATION RATE</div>
    </div>
    <div class="anchor-divider"></div>
    <div class="anchor-text">
      <h2>The Implementation Gap</h2>
      <p>Of all AI models published in peer-reviewed literature, fewer than 5% have undergone prospective clinical validation in real-world settings &mdash; despite 692 FDA-cleared AI/ML medical devices as of 2023.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="anchor-divider"></div>
    <div class="anchor-right">
      <div class="sub-num">692</div>
      <div class="sub-label">FDA-cleared AI/ML<br>medical devices (2023)</div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- BACKGROUND -->
  <div class="bg-section">
    <div class="bg-text">
      <h3>Background &amp; Motivation</h3>
      <p>The integration of AI into clinical decision-making is one of the most rapidly evolving frontiers in digital health. A growing body of evidence from over 400 studies examines how machine-learning-based clinical decision support systems perform across diagnostic, prognostic, and therapeutic domains. Despite promising results in controlled research settings, translation into routine clinical practice remains limited.</p>
      <div class="bg-highlight">Understanding the magnitude of this gap &mdash; and the factors driving it &mdash; is essential for researchers, clinicians, regulators, and health-system leaders.</div>
    </div>
    <div class="bg-stats">
      <div class="bg-stat-card">
        <div class="bsv">400+</div>
        <div class="bsl">Studies reviewed</div>
      </div>
      <div class="bg-stat-card">
        <div class="bsv">4</div>
        <div class="bsl">Clinical domains</div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- MAIN CONTENT GRID -->
  <div class="content-grid">

    <!-- Diagnostic Performance -->
    <div class="section teal-border">
      <div class="section-tag">Findings</div>
      <h3>Diagnostic Performance</h3>
      <p>AI-assisted diagnosis demonstrated substantially higher sensitivity compared to clinician-only workflows, with the largest gains in medical imaging.</p>

      <div class="sens-compare">
        <div class="sens-box ai-box">
          <div class="sens-val">87.5%</div>
          <div class="sens-lbl">AI Sensitivity</div>
        </div>
        <div class="sens-vs">vs</div>
        <div class="sens-box clin-box">
          <div class="sens-val">78.2%</div>
          <div class="sens-lbl">Clinician Sensitivity</div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="metric-cards">
        <div class="metric-card">
          <div class="mc-value teal">93.1%</div>
          <div class="mc-label">AI Specificity</div>
        </div>
        <div class="metric-card">
          <div class="mc-value">91.4%</div>
          <div class="mc-label">Clinician Specificity</div>
        </div>
        <div class="metric-card">
          <div class="mc-value amber">32%</div>
          <div class="mc-label">Faster Turnaround</div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Domain Table -->
    <div class="section navy-border">
      <div class="section-tag">By Domain</div>
      <h3>Performance Across Specialties</h3>
      <p>Deep learning architectures consistently outperformed individual readers, with the greatest gains in radiology and pathology.</p>
      <table class="domain-table">
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th>Domain</th>
            <th>AI Sensitivity</th>
            <th>Clinician Sensitivity</th>
            <th>AI Specificity</th>
            <th>Clinician Specificity</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <td><strong>Radiology</strong></td>
            <td class="highlight-cell">89.3%</td>
            <td>79.8%</td>
            <td>94.0%</td>
            <td>92.1%</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><strong>Dermatology</strong></td>
            <td class="highlight-cell">86.7%</td>
            <td>76.5%</td>
            <td>91.8%</td>
            <td>89.4%</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><strong>Ophthalmology</strong></td>
            <td class="highlight-cell">90.1%</td>
            <td>82.3%</td>
            <td>95.2%</td>
            <td>93.7%</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><strong>Pathology</strong></td>
            <td class="highlight-cell">84.0%</td>
            <td>74.1%</td>
            <td>91.5%</td>
            <td>90.0%</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <!-- Safety & Bias -->
    <div class="section red-border">
      <div class="section-tag">Safety &amp; Equity</div>
      <h3>Bias Assessment: An Alarming Gap</h3>
      <p>Safety and equity remain critically underexamined. Performance disparities of 5&ndash;12 percentage points affect underrepresented populations.</p>

      <div class="warning-box">
        <div class="warning-number">18%</div>
        <div class="warning-content">
          <strong>Only 18% of studies reported any formal bias assessment</strong>
          <p>Demographic-stratified performance reporting across age, sex, ethnicity, and socioeconomic status remains alarmingly rare. Dermatology AI trained predominantly on lighter skin tones showed the most significant disparities.</p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="metric-cards" style="margin-top:14px;">
        <div class="metric-card">
          <div class="mc-value" style="color:#dc2626;">&lt;1%</div>
          <div class="mc-label">Adverse events in<br>prospective trials</div>
        </div>
        <div class="metric-card">
          <div class="mc-value" style="color:#d97706;">5&ndash;12pp</div>
          <div class="mc-label">Sensitivity gap for<br>underrepresented groups</div>
        </div>
      </div>
      <p style="margin-top:10px;font-size:15px;color:#94a3b8;">Under-reporting suspected. Standardised safety-reporting frameworks for AI-CDSS remain absent.</p>
    </div>

    <!-- Barriers -->
    <div class="section amber-border">
      <div class="section-tag">Barriers</div>
      <h3>Why AI Stalls at the Bedside</h3>
      <p>Key barriers preventing AI models from reaching routine clinical use:</p>
      <div class="barriers">
        <div class="barrier-item">
          <div class="barrier-icon">1</div>
          <div>
            <strong>Data Infrastructure</strong>
            <span>Fragmented EHR systems and lack of interoperability standards</span>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="barrier-item">
          <div class="barrier-icon">2</div>
          <div>
            <strong>Regulatory Uncertainty</strong>
            <span>Evolving frameworks lagging behind AI development pace</span>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="barrier-item">
          <div class="barrier-icon">3</div>
          <div>
            <strong>Clinician Trust</strong>
            <span>Insufficient explainability and transparency of model outputs</span>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="barrier-item">
          <div class="barrier-icon">4</div>
          <div>
            <strong>Economic Evidence</strong>
            <span>Limited cost-effectiveness analyses for health-system investment</span>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="barrier-item" style="grid-column:1/-1;">
          <div class="barrier-icon">5</div>
          <div>
            <strong>Workflow Integration</strong>
            <span>Poor alignment of AI tools with existing clinical workflows</span>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Recommendations (full width) -->
    <div class="section teal-border full-width">
      <div class="section-tag">Path Forward</div>
      <h3>Recommendations &amp; Future Directions</h3>
      <p style="margin-bottom:8px;">Closing the gap between AI research promise and clinical reality requires coordinated action across the research, regulatory, and healthcare delivery ecosystem.</p>
      <div class="rec-list">
        <div class="rec-item">
          <div class="rec-num">1</div>
          <p><strong>Mandate prospective validation</strong> before clinical deployment, including multi-site and diverse-population trials.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="rec-item">
          <div class="rec-num">2</div>
          <p><strong>Standardise bias auditing</strong> using demographic-stratified performance reporting as a publication requirement.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="rec-item">
          <div class="rec-num">3</div>
          <p><strong>Develop shared safety-reporting registries</strong> analogous to pharmacovigilance systems for AI-CDSS.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="rec-item">
          <div class="rec-num">4</div>
          <p><strong>Invest in interoperability</strong> through adoption of open data standards (FHIR, OMOP).</p>
        </div>
        <div class="rec-item rec-center">
          <div class="rec-num">5</div>
          <p><strong>Prioritise explainability research</strong> to build clinician and patient trust in AI-augmented care.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

  </div>

  <!-- FOOTER -->
  <div class="footer">
    <strong>The Lancet Digital Health</strong> &mdash; AI in Clinical Medicine Symposium &nbsp;&bull;&nbsp; Systematic review of 400+ studies &nbsp;&bull;&nbsp; Source: The Lancet Digital Health (2023)
  </div>

</div>
</body>
